  [1mThe fork:[0m the runtime and how much framework. This is the least reversible choice in the whole project — it fixes the
  language, the deployment story, and what libraries exist for the parts that get hard later (background jobs, push,
  real-time).

  - [1mNode + TypeScript, thin server (Fastify/Express + SQLite), vanilla frontend[0m — Buys: no build step to fight, small
    surface, SQLite means zero infra; TS gives you real types on the notification payloads, which is where bugs live.
    Costs: you hand-roll routing/auth/state on the client as it grows. Cheap to add a framework later; expensive to
    remove one.
  - [1mFull-stack framework (Next.js / SvelteKit / Remix)[0m — Buys: routing, server actions, and a client data layer out of
    the box; real-time and auth have well-worn recipes. Costs: a lot of machinery for a task list, and framework
    conventions will shape the notification design more than you'd like. Hard to back out of.
  - [1mPython + FastAPI + SQLite[0m — Buys: matches the Python toolchain in your global CLAUDE.md (micromamba/uv, ruff, mypy),
    so tooling is already settled and you're on familiar ground. Costs: two languages once the frontend grows past
    vanilla JS; async background work is workable but less ergonomic than in Node.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m Node + TypeScript, thin server, SQLite. A notification system is mostly a data-modeling and
  event-plumbing problem, and a thin server keeps that plumbing visible instead of hiding it behind framework
  conventions — which matters because the seams are exactly what we want to learn from in the slice. One language across
  client and server also means the event/payload types are shared rather than duplicated.

  Worth noting your global CLAUDE.md is Python-heavy; if this project should live in that toolchain, say so and FastAPI
  is a perfectly good answer.
